  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nModel ensembles have changed little from yesterday's runs showing \nthe peak of the ridge and temperatures occurring on Saturday before \nthe ridge weakens Sunday into Monday. NBM foreast highs have come \ndown a degree or so from yesterday, but it is hard to find a good \nreason why. Forecast confidence remains high that some daily heat \nrecords are likely to be broken this weekend with southeast CA lower \ndeserts reaching 115-120 degrees to 113-117 degrees across the \nArizona lower deserts. To give some prospective, Phoenix has only \nreached 116 degrees or higher 18 times after July 30th, half of \nwhich occurred since 2023 and only reached 117 degrees or higher 6 \ntimes. The probability of KPHX hitting at least 116 degrees Friday-\nSunday is 20%, 67%, and 35% respectively. For Saturday, the NBM also \nshows a 20-25% probability of tying the all-time hottest temperature \nin August of 118 degrees which occurred August 7, 2025. We will have \nsome relief from the extreme heat at night, more so across the \nwestern deserts where lows should still dip into the mid to upper \n80s. The bulk of the Phoenix metro should have lows only in a 90-95 \ndegree range Friday-Sunday. \n\nAny potential rain chances over the weekend are trending even lower \nwith guidance mostly showing any decent moisture advection holding \noff until late Sunday into Monday. Some members still show some \nimproved chances over the eastern Arizona high terrain convection as \nearly as Sunday with potential for outflows into the Phoenix area \nSunday evening, but it seems more likely that will not occur until \nMonday at the earliest. Guidance does agree on the ridge noticeably \nweakening by Monday with H5 heights dropping to 594-596dm, but the \nresidual heat over the region is expected to keep extreme heat \nconditions around through Monday. The ridge center should also begin \nto drift back to the northeast, closer to the Four Corners area by \naround next Tuesday. If and when this happens, it should open up \nportions of the region for better moisture advection out of the \nsoutheast. Monsoon storm chances are expected to gradually improve \ngoing into the middle of next week, while NBM forecast highs drop \nback to within a few degrees of normals.",
